WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.560
- Hey there, "Two Cents" viewer.

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:02.430
Before we get started,

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:06.750
just a little bit of legal
housekeeping. (clearing throat)

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:10.260
By watching this video, you
are agreeing to the following.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.330
You shall not copy, transcribe,

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:16.380
or publicly display this episode
(except at an awards show).

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:18.870
You may not disparage
"Two Cents" or its hosts

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.250
in any capacity.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.410
And that includes comments
about Philip's hair,

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:23.970
or lack thereof.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.280
You must subscribe to
the "Two Cents" channel

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:27.720
and click the notifications bell

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.240
and become a patron on our Patreon page.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.620
Link in the description.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.210
You will tell at least five friends

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.610
that "Two Cents" is your
favorite show of all time,

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:36.600
and if they don't watch it,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.060
you won't be their friend anymore.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.530
By continuing to play this video,

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.200
you are agreeing to be bound
by the aforementioned terms

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.170
and conditions of this end
user license agreement.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.700
For those of you who are still here,

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.490
thank you for being such
disturbingly loyal fans,

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.723
but alas, this is not
remotely enforceable.

00:00:53.723 --> 00:00:56.374
(button beeping)
This, however, is.

00:00:56.374 --> 00:00:58.957
(lively music)

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.890
- As a CFP, I would ordinarily
never advise someone

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:09.960
to sign a contract that they haven't read.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.430
But when it comes to EULAs,

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.560
or End User License Agreements,

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.120
we don't really have a choice.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.610
- If you wanna participate
in modern society,

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.470
you probably have to click, "I agree,"

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.300
at least a dozen times a month.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.340
And the idea that any of us would

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.920
or could actually read all the terms

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.990
and conditions is ludicrous.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.600
iTunes' agreement is
longer than "Macbeth,"

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.460
just to buy a $2 song.

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:34.657
- And even though we all
check the box that says,

00:01:34.657 --> 00:01:37.080
"I have read and understand these terms,"

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.840
most people would need an
extended session with a lawyer

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.090
to really know what they're agreeing to.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.550
One study estimated that
the average American

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.740
would have to devote an entire month

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.350
to properly understand all
the terms and conditions

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.600
they agree to each year.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:52.860
- If you need any more evidence

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.480
that people don't read these things,

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.760
one games retailer snuck a clause

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.320
into their terms and conditions,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.340
giving the company "a
non-transferrable option to claim

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.790
for now and forevermore,
your immortal soul."

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.370
Over 7,000 people clicked, "Agree."

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.130
22,000 people committed to 1,000 hours

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.650
of community service in
exchange for free wifi.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.380
And when a software maker promised $1,000

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.840
to the first person who noticed the offer

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.910
in the fine print of their EULA,

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:23.760
it took four months for
someone to claim it.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.070
- Most of us have just accepted all this

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.260
as an annoying fact of modern life,

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.990
a technocratic oddity of the internet age.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.810
But EULAs are just the
latest weapon in a battle

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:36.150
that's been going on for 100 years,

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.270
a battle against ownership rights.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.880
- In 1904, the publisher Bobbs-Merrill

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.430
printed a small disclaimer
inside each copy

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.410
of the novel, "The Castaway."

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:49.950
It read, "The price of this
book is one dollar net.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:53.190
No dealer is licensed to
sell it at a less price,

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.560
and a sale at a less price will be treated

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.930
as an infringement of the copyright."

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:59.130
- [Philip] Like most publishers,

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.320
Bobbs-Merrill saw used bookstores

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.090
as a threat to their business.

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:05.910
In a perfect world, everyone
who wanted to read their book

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:09.630
would have to buy their own
brand new copy at full price,

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:11.850
and they thought they could
force people to do that

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:14.850
by invoking copyright law.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:15.750
- It didn't work.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.730
The courts struck down their lawsuit

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:21.000
because they saw it as an
attempt to attach servitude

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.590
to personal property.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.510
Servitude refers to conditions

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:27.780
or obligations that are
tied to a sold property.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:29.400
It's most common in real estate,

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:32.460
like mandating what kind
of structures can be built

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:33.750
on a piece of land.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:36.780
But it's not allowed when it
comes to personal property.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.520
You can't sell someone a hammer

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.710
and then tell them what
they can build with it.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:42.420
The new owner can use their property

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.010
how and when they want.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:46.020
- These rights of ownership were codified

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.900
in the Copyright Act of 1909.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:50.790
It created protections for the owners

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:53.400
of intellectual property
like authors and publishers,

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.350
but it also carved out some rights

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:57.990
for the buyers of individual copies.

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:00.330
According to the Act, if you buy a book,

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:02.820
you can't make duplicates and sell them.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.400
You can't charge audiences
to hear a public reading

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.590
or sell your own sequel.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.080
You can, however, resell
the book if you want to,

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.930
or lend it to a friend,
or give it as a gift.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:14.220
- Over the next hundred years,

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:15.330
those rights of ownership

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:18.150
have been relentlessly
targeted by IP owners.

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:21.810
Record companies tried to
shut down used music shops.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:24.720
Movie studios tried to
criminalize the VCR,

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.450
and video game developers
tried to stop the sale

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:29.010
of used games.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:31.860
While digital technology has
created some real threats

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.020
of illegal duplication and piracy,

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.630
most of these efforts are about control,

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:39.750
trying to control how people use a product

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.850
after they've bought it.

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:45.510
- Time and again, the
courts sided with consumers,

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:47.370
ruling that unless people were making

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.440
and distributing illegal copies,

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:51.210
they were free to do whatever they wanted

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.830
with their individual copies.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:54.630
But with the digital revolution,

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:58.080
IP holders found a way around the law:

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.230
ask consumers to voluntarily sign away

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:04.740
their rights to ownership as
a condition of the purchase,

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.950
the End User License Agreement.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:10.177
- Even though e-sellers use language like,

00:05:10.177 --> 00:05:13.050
"Buy now," and "Own it in 4K,"

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:15.240
if you actually read the terms,

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:18.990
you'll find that you don't
actually own the thing you buy,

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:20.700
you're just getting a license

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:24.780
or permission to use it
in certain limited ways.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:28.230
Most EULAs don't allow you to
lend or gift your purchases

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:31.440
and dictate what devices
you can use them on.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:34.290
Some prohibit any kind of disparagement,

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:37.110
which could even mean a negative review.

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:38.850
And many reserve the right

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:43.080
to revoke your license at
any time without a refund.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.000
- Besides the loss of ownership rights,

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.100
the shift from buying to licensing

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.230
has other economic downsides.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:51.750
Artists make much less from streaming

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.580
than they did from physical media

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:56.670
and lack that tactile
connection to their fans.

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:58.710
The death of used media shops may mean

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:01.710
that lower income audiences
will have fewer ways

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:03.270
to discover music and movies,

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:06.030
which, in the long run,
is bad for the industry.

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:08.310
And if switching from
one platform to another,

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:10.710
like say PlayStation to Xbox

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:13.470
means you'll lose your entire library,

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:16.380
customers are so locked in
that there's little motivation

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.810
for companies to innovate or compete.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:20.280
- Nevertheless, EULAs have become

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.170
so lucrative for businesses

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:24.870
that they're looking to
expand them beyond IP

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:27.900
into more tangible
purchases like vehicles,

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:29.970
appliances, and electronics.

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:33.900
Anything with a processor can
be loaded with simple software

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:34.920
that allows the seller

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:38.070
to control its functioning after purchase.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:39.660
Coffee makers can be programmed

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:42.540
to only brew certain brands of coffee.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:45.030
Cars and tractors can be encrypted

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:47.490
to prohibit even simple repairs

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.950
unless at an official dealership.

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:52.770
And printers can be remotely
bricked by the company

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:56.010
if you're late paying the
toner subscription fee.

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:59.100
- Ordinarily, this kind of
servitude would be illegal.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:02.250
The law clearly gives
buyers the right to alter,

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:05.100
repair, or resell whatever they own.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.830
But though you may own the coffee maker,

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:10.770
you don't own the
software installed on it,

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:12.120
you're just licensing it,

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:14.130
which means any attempt to subvert

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:16.950
or bypass it is a breach of contract.

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:18.330
If things keep going this way,

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:19.980
someday, you could get sued

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:22.680
just for loaning your
lawnmower to a neighbor.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:24.900
- But there is a backlash gaining momentum

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:27.390
known as the Right to Repair movement.

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:29.310
More than half the states are considering

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:30.930
or have passed bills

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:33.270
that prohibit companies
from inserting these kinds

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:35.820
of digital restrictions
into physical products.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:39.120
And unlike almost every
other issue in America,

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.370
this one is uniting people from both sides

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:42.930
of the political spectrum.

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:45.390
Turns out that the vast
majority of Americans believe

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:47.460
that you should be able
to do what you want

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:49.110
with the stuff you buy.

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:50.850
- Maybe with a little political action,

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:52.770
we can stop a dystopian future

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:55.530
where we're all subscribers
rather than owners.

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:59.100
But until then, EULAs are
enforceable contracts.

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:01.320
Even though consumers
have no way around them,

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:03.960
many courts have affirmed
that it is indeed possible

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:07.440
to waive your hundred year
old rights of ownership

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:09.240
just by clicking on a box.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:10.830
- And unless you're willing to opt out

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:13.920
of all popular culture,
that's what you'll have to do.

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:17.250
But don't kid yourself,
you're not buying anything.

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:19.830
They're just letting
you use it for a while.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:21.390
- [Both] And that's our two cents.

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:24.240
- Before you go, we wanna
tell you about a new show

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:28.230
on PBS Origins, "A People's
History of Native America,"

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:30.930
hosted by comedian and
actor Tai' Leclaire,

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:33.840
it's a miniseries that explores
the current social climate

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:34.950
in Native America,

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:37.470
diving deep into the
history of tribal nations

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:38.850
and the external factors

00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:41.340
that have shaped
contemporary circumstances.

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:42.870
There's a link in the description.

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:44.321
We hope you enjoy.

00:08:44.321 --> 00:08:46.904
(lively music)

